The search for "firebase 10 firestore cdn modular import getFirestore doc getDoc setDoc collection getDocs" returned the following summary:
To use the **Firebase 10 Modular SDK** directly via a **CDN** in a browser script (`type="module"`), you need to import from absolute URL paths pointing to the official Google Hosted CDN (`gstatic.com`)[1][2].

Here is a complete template demonstrating how to initialize Firebase and use `getFirestore`, `collection`, `doc`, `setDoc`, `getDoc`, and `getDocs`[3][4][1]:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Firebase 10 CDN Modular Example</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Firebase 10 Firestore CDN Demo</h1>

    <script type="module">
        // 1. Import Firebase App and Firestore modules from the official CDN (version 10)
        import { initializeApp } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.0/firebase-app.js";
        import { 
            getFirestore, 
            collection, 
            doc, 
            setDoc, 
            getDoc, 
            getDocs 
        } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.0/firebase-firestore.js";

        // 2. Your web app's Firebase configuration
        const firebaseConfig = {
            apiKey: "YOUR_API_KEY",
            authDomain: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID.firebaseapp.com",
            projectId: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID",
            storageBucket: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID.appspot.com",
            messagingSenderId: "YOUR_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID",
            appId: "YOUR_APP_ID"
        };

        // 3. Initialize Firebase and Firestore
        const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
        const db = getFirestore(app);

        async function runFirestoreOperations() {
            try {
                // --- SET DOC (Write/Overwrite a document with a specific ID) ---
                const userDocRef = doc(db, "users", "user_123");
                await setDoc(userDocRef, {
                    name: "Jane Doe",
                    role: "Admin",
                    createdAt: new Date()
                });
                console.log("Document successfully written!");

                // --- GET DOC (Fetch a single document by ID) ---
                const docSnap = await getDoc(userDocRef);
                if (docSnap.exists()) {
                    console.log("Document data:", docSnap.data());
                } else {
                    console.log("No such document!");
                }

                // --- GET DOCS (Fetch all documents in a collection) ---
                const usersCollectionRef = collection(db, "users");
                const querySnapshot = await getDocs(usersCollectionRef);

                querySnapshot.forEach((document) => {
                    console.log(`${document.id} =>`, document.data());
                });

            } catch (error) {
                console.error("Error performing Firestore operation: ", error);
            }
        }

        // Execute the functions
        runFirestoreOperations();
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Key Rules for Firebase 10 Modular CDN:
1. **`type="module"`**: The script tag must include `type="module"` because the modular CDN architecture relies on standard JavaScript ES modules[1].
2. **Explicit URLs**: Always use matching version numbers (e.g., `/10.12.0/`) across all imported packages (`firebase-app.js` and `firebase-firestore.js`).
3. **Function-based syntax**: Unlike version 8 (namespaced syntax like `db.collection().doc()`), Firebase 10 uses standalone modular functions where you pass the reference container first (e.g., `doc(db, "collectionName", "docId")` or `collection(db, "collectionName")`)[3][2].

